{{Quote|Children of the Watch are a cult of religious zealots that broke away from Mandalorian society. Their goal was to reestablish the ancient way.|A biased Bo-Katan Kryze describes the Children to Din Djarin|<ref name="Chapter 11" />}}
The '''Children of the Watch''', also known as the '''Followers of the Way''',<ref name="SWE">''[[Star Wars Encyclopedia: The Comprehensive Guide to the Star Wars Galaxy]]''</ref> were<ref name="Chapter 11" /> an orthodox<ref name="SW Book">''[[The Star Wars Book]]''</ref> [[religion]]<ref name="Character Encyclopedia">''[[Star Wars: Character Encyclopedia, Updated and Expanded Edition]]''</ref> of [[Mandalorian]]s who followed the [[Way of the Mandalore]]. Its adherents were characterized as religious zealots by [[Lady]] [[Bo-Katan Kryze]] and others in mainstream Mandalorian society, who had abandoned the ancient faith long before the [[Galactic Civil War]].<ref name="Chapter 11" /> The Children of the Watch were primarily descendants of [[Death Watch]] members.<ref name="100 Objects">''[[Star Wars 100 Objects]]''</ref> Due to being cloistered on [[Concordia]], they survived the [[Great Purge of Mandalore]].<ref name="BobaChapter 5" />

[[The Tribe]] were members of the Children of the Watch,<ref name="The Tribe note" /> but at least one of its members, [[Din Djarin]], was unaware that there was anything unusual about their beliefs and practices. Instead, Djarin believed that all Mandalorians were like them until he met Bo-Katan Kryze and her [[Mandalorian warrior]]s, [[Axe Woves]] and [[Koska Reeves]].<ref name="Chapter 11" /> Those Mandalorians, Kryze especially, considered the Children of the Watch to be a cult that had greatly contributed to the divided nature and weakness of their people.<ref name="Chapter 17" /> The Children of the Watch did not follow the custom of the [[Darksaber]], and station or bloodline was not important to them.<ref name="Chapter 23">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 23: The Spies}}</ref> After spending time with the Children, Kryze decided the Children were not a cult, but simply a group interested in ancient ways of their ancestors.<ref name="Chapter 22">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 22: Guns for Hire}}</ref> With the [[Conflict on Mandalore|reconquest of Mandalore]], the Children put aside their obsession with the Way to work with [[Mandalorian resistance|Kryze's forces]].<ref name="Chapter 24">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 24: The Return}}</ref>

{{Mando-stub}}

==Behind the scenes==
In the French version of ''[[Star Wars: The Mandalorian]]'', Children of the Watch is translated as ''Les Héritiers de la Death Watch'' (The Heirs of the [[Death Watch]]).<ref name="Chapter 11 French">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 11: The Heiress}} {{C|French audio/subtitles}}</ref> Together with the fact that Djarin was raised as a [[foundling]] in the Tribe<ref name="The Tribe note" /> after being rescued from a [[Confederacy of Independent Systems|Separatist]] [[attack on Aq Vetina]] by Death Watch members,<ref name="Chapter 8">{{TheMandalorian|Chapter 8: Redemption}}</ref> and the fact that the Children of the Watch adhered to the Way of the Mandalore and other customs abandoned by the [[New Mandalorians]],<ref name="Chapter 11" /> this suggests that the Children of the Watch may be an offshoot of the Death Watch. ''[[Star Wars 100 Objects]]'' later confirmed most survivors of the [[Night of a Thousand Tears]] were descendants of Death Watch.<ref name="100 Objects" />

